<p>Russian President Vladimir Putin said on May 9 that he thought the war in Ukraine was “coming to an end,” after Moscow held its most scaled-back Victory Day parade in years and hours after he vowed victory over Kyiv. Putin said he was willing to discuss new European security arrangements and named former German Chancellor Gerhard Schröder as his preferred interlocutor. He said he could meet Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in a third country only after final peace terms were agreed. The Kremlin repeated its conditions. Presidential aide Yury Ushakov said peace talks would remain stalled until Ukrainian troops withdraw from Donbas, and that further rounds would not change Moscow’s position without that step. Ushakov said Moscow and Washington remain in phone contact and that President Trump’s envoys, Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner, could visit Russia soon. Kyiv said Russian forces were not observing the lull along the front and that Ukrainian forces were responding in kind.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6WbT98P8XWoPzLTuXUwyPCmQ4M7eBogGFFbaz_fswD8NXPuOl-NYl5CTOC0vIoQcGvjiMKefoNqZZGnPR95d0MsLU7UodRu1cNNXF5DLkA' alt='Firefighters work next to destroyed vehicles at the site of a Russian missile strike in Kyiv, Ukraine.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Ceasefire scope: The temporary three-day, U.S.-brokered truce was tied to the Victory Day period and, according to Libertv, helped Moscow hold the event without major incidents.</li><li>Parade limits: Moscow’s May 9 parade did not feature the usual intercontinental ballistic missiles, tanks and missile systems, instead showing military hardware on giant screens opposite the Kremlin walls.</li><li>Battlefield pace: Slovak reporting said Russia’s advance on the front had slowed markedly, and battlefield analyses pointed to growing problems for the Russian army despite Moscow’s claims of progress.</li><li>Direct reference: Coverage noted that Putin made a rare public mention of Zelenskyy by name while discussing possible talks, a departure from his usual public language.</li></ul><blockquote>I think that the matter is coming to an end - Vladimir Putin</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Vladimir Putin: Russia’s president argued that Moscow’s core objective was security and tied the conflict to what he described as Western escalation and NATO enlargement after the Cold War. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330458744/putin-says-he-thinks-russia-ukraine-war-is-coming-to-an-end'>Aaj TV</a>)</li><li>Yury Ushakov: The Russian presidential aide said Ukraine knew Moscow expected a Donbas withdrawal and claimed Kyiv would take that step “sooner or later,” even if more negotiating rounds were held. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/639840-kremlin-condition-settle-ukraine-conflict/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Volodymyr Zelenskyy: Zelenskyy said Putin’s statement that he was ready for real meetings meant the sides needed to find a workable format. (<a href='https://www.radiosvoboda.org/a/news-zelensky-rosiiska-armiia-ne-dotrymuietsia-tyshi-na-fronti/33753848.html'>Radio Svoboda</a>)</li><li>German government circles: Sources in German government circles characterized the Schröder proposal as another apparent offer in Russia’s hybrid strategy rather than a genuine EU mediation plan. (<a href='https://kurier.at/politik/ausland/ukraine-krieg-russland-putin-ende-selenskij/403158642'>Kurier</a>)</li><li>Kirill Rogov: The Russian analyst told TV Rain that the Kremlin appeared to be trying to present Putin as wanting peace while portraying the West as blocking it. (<a href='https://radiomoldova.md/p/76188/analist-rus-kremlinul-incearca-sa-transmita-ca-putin-vrea-pacea-iar-occidentul-se-opune'>Radio Moldova</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/639840-kremlin-condition-settle-ukraine-conflict/'>Kremlin explains impasse in Ukraine peace talks</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/5/10/putin-hints-at-ending-russias-war-i